From 93611ad2888b2514adb3db1970bc8be12b098ce1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: srymsjackson <jstrazzo22@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 21:28:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Sign /billing/checkout links instead of trusting raw
 business_id/plan params

/billing/checkout previously took business_id and plan as plain, editable
query params: /billing/checkout?business_id=5&plan=basic. Rate limiting
(already in place) slowed down brute-force enumeration, but did nothing
about the more direct problem -- anyone holding a legitimate link could
just edit it: change ?plan=basic to ?plan=pro, or change ?business_id=5 to
?business_id=6 and have a completed payment activate a different business
than the one the payer intended to pay for.

New app/billing_tokens.py signs business_id + plan together into one opaque
token (itsdangerous, same approach as SESSION_SECRET) with a 30-day
expiry -- long enough that a link sitting in an unread SMS still works, not
so long that a months-old link from an abandoned onboarding still does.
CHECKOUT_LINK_SECRET is its own secret, falling back through SESSION_SECRET
then DASHBOARD_KEY with a warning at each level, same layered pattern as
app/auth.py -- keeps checkout-link forgery and session forgery as separate
blast radii.

/billing/checkout now takes ?token=... instead of business_id/plan; those
two are no longer read from the query string at all, so there's nothing
left to hand-edit. An invalid signature, a tampered token, an expired
token, and a valid token pointing at a deleted business_id all now return
the identical 400 -- no distinguishing signal about which failure mode
happened. /onboarding/create generates token-based links.

Also updated the existing /billing/checkout rate-limit test
(tests/test_rate_limiting.py), which was still hitting the old
?business_id=1&plan=basic URL -- it happened to still pass (it only checked
the 429 boundary, never the response body), but was testing a URL shape the
app no longer accepts. Rewrote it to mint a real token against a real
Business row.

Tests: tests/test_billing_checkout_tokens.py (10 new tests) -- token
round-trip, tampering (bit-flip breaks the signature), confirms the route
never reads business_id/plan from the query string even when both are
supplied alongside a valid token, identical error response for
invalid-signature vs valid-token-deleted-business, and onboarding actually
emits ?token= links now. Full suite: 100/100 passing. Verified
test_route_only_ever_uses_business_id_and_plan_from_the_verified_token
catches a regression by reintroducing a trusted business_id override
param and re-running (fails as expected).
